{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הנראה", :translit "ha-nir'eh", :gloss "the [one who] appeared"} {:he "ב' במסורה", :translit "bet be-masorah", :gloss "two in the Masorah"} {:he "הכא ואידך ביעקב", :translit "haka ve-idakh be-ya'akov", :gloss "here and the other in Jacob"} {:he "כשם שנראה לאברהם כשגלה כך נראה ליעקב כשגלה", :translit "k'shem she-nir'eh le-avraham k'she-galah kakh nir'eh le-ya'akov k'she-galah", :gloss "just as He appeared to Abraham when he went into exile, so He appeared to Jacob when he went into exile"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["הנראה"]}], :frame {:text "The root נראה (appeared) applied to God's self-revelation characterizes several patriarchal theophanies: God appears to Abraham (Genesis 12:7) and to Jacob (Genesis 35:9, 48:3). Jacob's own statement in Genesis 35:3 identifies God as 'ha-El ha-nir'eh eilekha' (the God who appeared to you), referring back to his Bethel vision. The phrase 'ka'asher galal Avraham' describes Abraham's departure from his land as a divinely-commanded journey (Genesis 12:1), while Jacob's departure to Charan is driven by flight from Esau (Genesis 28:10).", :sources ["Genesis 12:1" "Genesis 12:7" "Genesis 28:10" "Genesis 35:3" "Genesis 35:9" "Genesis 48:3"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["הנראה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "הנראה", :refs ["Genesis 12:7" "Genesis 35:1" "1 Kings 11:9" "Daniel 8:1"], :n 4, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:05:30.562262088Z", :model "glm-5.2"}